.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt REFLASH 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm reflash
.Nd write a stream to a raw device or hash a device range
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl -verify
.Ar DEVICE OFFSET BYTES
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command copies an image from standard input to a raw device.
The
.Ar OFFSET
and
.Ar BYTES
operands give the starting offset and declared length in bytes.
Obtain the target partition's start and size from the machine's partition
table, reported in
.Pa /proc/disk .
The offset is its starting sector multiplied by 512.
The length is the image's uncompressed length and must fit the partition.
The range is checked against the device before any input is read;
this check does not establish which partition the range occupies.
.Pp
The length must be nonzero.
The offset and length must be whole numbers of sectors, and the range
must fit the device.
Offset 0 is permitted for a whole-disk write.
The input must contain exactly the declared number of bytes.
An early end or data beyond that length is a transfer failure.
Input is gathered into batches of 2048 sectors before writing.
.Pp
The
.Fl p
option prints progress on standard error every sixteen mebibytes and
at the end:
.Dl reflash: N of N bytes, P%
.Pp
The
.Fl -verify
option writes nothing.
It reads the specified device range and prints its SHA-256 on standard
output:
.Dl reflash: sha256 HEX of N bytes at OFFSET
Failure to read the complete range is an error.
A matching digest establishes the contents of the supplied range;
it does not establish that the offset is correct.
With
.Fl p ,
read progress is reported as:
.Dl reflash: read N of N bytes, P%
.Pp
Only a kernel built for recovery permits writes to whole-disk or
partition nodes.
Other kernels refuse the device write with
.Er EPERM .
The refusal is reported without a partial write.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is 0 on success, 1 for a refusal or failed transfer,
and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The sealed disk layout has a 64 MiB FAT boot partition at byte 1048576,
a 64 MiB recovery partition at byte 68157440 (sector 133120), and
a 1 GiB ext2 root image at byte 136314880.
Use the offsets reported by the machine for the disk being written.
.Pp
To rewrite the recovery partition while running the disk's recovery
entry, first extract its image on the host:
.Dl dd if=build/images/rach2-sealed.disk of=recovery.img bs=512 skip=133120 count=131072
Send it over SSH:
.Dl gzip -c recovery.img | ssh -i $KEY root@ADDRESS 'gunzip | reflash -p /dev/disk0 68157440 67108864'
On the recovery system, read back and hash the same range:
.Dl reflash --verify /dev/disk0 68157440 67108864
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
An incorrect operand count prints:
.Dl usage: reflash [-p] [--verify] DEVICE OFFSET BYTES
A nonnumeric offset or length is reported as
.Ql reflash: 'X' is not an offset
or
.Ql reflash: 'X' is not a length .
.Pp
A zero length is refused with
.Ql a length of zero writes nothing .
An unaligned offset or length, or a range past the device's end, is
refused before input is read.
Bounds errors include the device size as
.Ql (DEVICE has N bytes) .
.Pp
Device errors are reported as
.Ql reflash: cannot open DEVICE: ERROR ,
.Ql reflash: cannot measure DEVICE: ERROR ,
.Ql reflash: cannot seek DEVICE to OFFSET: ERROR ,
or
.Ql reflash: DEVICE did not close cleanly: ERROR .
A failed transfer reports the amount written as
.Ql (N of N bytes written at OFFSET) .
An incomplete verification reports:
.Dl reflash: DEVICE stopped answering after N of N bytes
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8
